The inclusion $F\subseteq K$ makes $K$ a <vector space> over $F$ by its own addition and multiplication by elements of $F$. The extension is finite when this <vector space> has a finite <basis>. Its <degree of a field extension> is
$$
\boxed{[K:F]=\dim_FK.}
$$
Being a <finite field extension> concerns vector-space dimension, not the cardinality of the underlying field.

= ii
{parent=B1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Since $K\subseteq L$ and $L/F$ is finite, an $F$-<linearly independent> set in $K$ remains independent in $L$, so $K/F$ is finite. A finite $F$-spanning set of $L$ also spans it over $K$, so $L/K$ is finite.

Take an $F$-<basis> $a_1,\ldots,a_m$ of $K$ and a $K$-<basis> $b_1,\ldots,b_n$ of $L$. Expanding coefficients twice shows that all products $a_ib_j$ span $L$ over $F$. If $\sum_{i,j}c_{ij}a_ib_j=0$ with $c_{ij}\in F$, independence of the $b_j$ forces $\sum_ic_{ij}a_i=0$ for each $j$, and then independence of the $a_i$ forces every coefficient zero. Thus these products form an $F$-<basis>, proving
$$
\boxed{[L:F]=[L:K][K:F].}
$$

= Solution
{parent=B1}

The <polynomial> $x^2-2$ is an <irreducible polynomial> over $\mathbb Q$, so $[\mathbb Q(\sqrt2):\mathbb Q]=2$. This intermediate field is real, so it does not contain $i$. Hence $x^2+1$ has no root in it and adjoining $i$ has degree two. The <tower law> gives
$$
\boxed{[\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,i):\mathbb Q]=4.}
$$
A <basis> is $1,\sqrt2,i,i\sqrt2$. Square-bracket adjoining notation in this algebraic example gives the same field as round-bracket adjoining notation.

