= Solution
{parent=D1}

For $x>0$ the geometric ratio $e^{-2x}$ has modulus less than one, and
$$
\sum_{m\geq0}(1-e^{-x})e^{-(2m+1)x}
=\frac{(1-e^{-x})e^{-x}}{1-e^{-2x}}=\frac1{e^x+1}.
$$
Every summand is nonnegative. The <monotone convergence theorem> therefore permits integration of the sum:
$$
\int_0^\infty\frac{dx}{e^x+1}
=\sum_{m\geq0}\left(\frac1{2m+1}-\frac1{2m+2}\right).
$$
The <integral> is finite because its integrand is bounded above by $e^{-x}$, whose <integral> is one. The displayed paired sums are the even partial sums of the <alternating harmonic series>; its odd partial sums differ by a term tending to zero. Thus the required equality holds with the usual series ordering. One can additionally evaluate it by $t=e^{-x}$:
$$
\boxed{\int_0^\infty\frac{dx}{e^x+1}=\int_0^1\frac{dt}{1+t}=\log2
=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}n.}
$$
Grouping into nonnegative pairs supplies the justified interchange; <absolute convergence> of the ungrouped integrated alternating series is not needed and would be false.

= D2
{c}
{parent=2004/D}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=D2}

The indicated <measurable function> is nonnegative. <Tonelli's theorem> applies without first assuming <integrability> and gives
$$
\int_{\mathbb R^2}h=\int_a^b\left(\int_1^\infty x^{-y}\,dx\right)dy
=\int_a^b\frac{dy}{y-1}=\log\frac{b-1}{a-1}<\infty.
$$
Thus $h$ is integrable. <Fubini's theorem> now allows the reverse order. For $x>1$,
$$
\int_a^bx^{-y}\,dy=\frac{x^{-a}-x^{-b}}{\log x}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\int_1^\infty\frac{x^{-a}-x^{-b}}{\log x}\,dx=\log\frac{b-1}{a-1}.}
$$
At $x=1$ the quotient has the removable limit $b-a$; that endpoint does not create an additional divergence.

