= Solution
{parent=E1}

A <metric space> is a set $X$ with a function $d:X\times X\to[0,\infty)$ satisfying $d(x,y)=0\iff x=y$, symmetry and the <triangle inequality>. A subset $U$ is open if every $x\in U$ has some open <open ball> $B(x,r)$ contained in $U$.

For finitely many <open sets> $U_1,\ldots,U_k$ and $x$ in their intersection, choose radii $r_j>0$ with $B(x,r_j)\subseteq U_j$. The positive minimum radius gives a ball contained in the intersection. Thus every <finite intersection> is open; the empty intersection is $X$, also open.

For distinct points put $\delta=d(x_1,x_2)>0$. The balls $B(x_1,\delta/3)$ and $B(x_2,\delta/3)$ are disjoint: a common point would make $d(x_1,x_2)<2\delta/3$ by the <triangle inequality>. This proves the <Hausdorff condition>.

A <singleton> $\{x\}$ is closed because each $y\ne x$ has a ball of radius $d(x,y)/2$ avoiding it, making its complement open. <Finite unions> of <closed sets> are closed, as their complements are <finite intersections> of <open sets>. Hence **every finite subset is closed**, including the empty subset.

= E2
{c}
{parent=2004/E}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=E2}

The <product topology> is generated by the <basis of a topology> of open rectangles $U\times V$, with $U$ open in $X$ and $V$ open in $Y$. For a fixed $y$,
$$
 i_y^{-1}(U\times V)=\begin{cases}U,&y\in V,\\\varnothing,&y\notin V.\end{cases}
$$
Both possibilities are open; checking this <basis of a topology> proves that $i_y$ is <continuous>. The analogous map $j_x:Y\to X\times Y$, $j_x(y)=(x,y)$, is <continuous> by the same argument.

If either factor is empty the product is empty and <connected>. Otherwise let $h:X\times Y\to\{0,1\}$ be <continuous>. For each $y$, $h\circ i_y$ is constant since $X$ is <connected>. Thus $h(x,y)=h(x_0,y)$ for any fixed $x_0\in X$. The right-hand side is $h\circ j_{x_0}$ and is constant in $y$ since $Y$ is <connected>. Therefore $h$ is constant on the whole product. The given characterization proves **$X\times Y$ is <connected>**.

