The <total derivative> is the unique <linear map> $Df_a:\mathbb R^m\to\mathbb R^n$ satisfying
$$
\boxed{f(a+h)=f(a)+Df_a(h)+o(\|h\|)\quad\text{as }h\to0.}
$$
This describes the best first-order linear approximation without choosing coordinates. The <chain rule> is
$$
\boxed{D(g\circ f)_a=Dg_{f(a)}\circ Df_a.}
$$
The evaluation point $f(a)$ in the outer <derivative> matters, particularly when composing a quadratic <matrix> map with the exponential.

= a
{parent=F1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Expand at a <matrix> increment $H$:
$$
(A+H)^t(A+H)=A^tA+H^tA+A^tH+H^tH.
$$
The last term is $O(\|H\|^2)$ in a <matrix norm>, so it contributes no <derivative>. Hence
$$
\boxed{Ds_A(H)=H^tA+A^tH.}
$$
This is a linear operator on <matrix> increments, not a scalar entrywise <derivative>.

= b
{parent=F1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The requested base point is the <identity matrix> $I$, not the zero <matrix>. Since $I$ commutes with every $H$,
$$
\exp(I+H)=\exp(I)\exp(H)=e[I+H+O(\|H\|^2)].
$$
The <matrix> <power series> justifies the quadratic remainder near zero. Consequently
$$
\boxed{D\exp_I(H)=eH.}
$$
At zero the <derivative> would instead be $H$; replacing the printed base point would lose the scalar factor.

= c
{parent=F1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Here $\exp(I)=eI$. Applying part (a) at that <matrix> gives $Ds_{eI}(K)=e(K^t+K)$, while part (b) gives $D\exp_I(H)=eH$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{D(s\circ\exp)_I(H)=Ds_{eI}(eH)=e^2(H^t+H).}
$$
Both the outer evaluation point and the inner <derivative> contribute a factor of $e$.

