Multiply the equation for $y_i$ by $y_j$ and the equation for $y_j$ by $y_i$, then subtract. The $q$ terms cancel and the <derivative> terms combine into a <Wronskian> boundary <derivative>:
$$
\frac d{dx}[p(y_jy_i'-y_iy_j')]+(\lambda_i-\lambda_j)wy_iy_j=0.
$$
Integrating over the interval eliminates the boundary term because both <eigenfunctions> vanish at both endpoints. Since their <eigenvalues> differ,
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1w(x)y_i(x)y_j(x)\,dx=0.}
$$
This is <orthogonality> in a <weighted inner product> in the regular Sturm-Liouville setting; the endpoint values must justify the <integration by parts>.

= ii
{parent=G1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $\lambda=0$, only a linear function can solve the equation, and zero endpoint values make it zero. If $\lambda<0$, the corresponding hyperbolic-sine solution also cannot vanish at both endpoints unless it is zero. For $\lambda=k^2>0$, the first endpoint gives $y=C\sin kx$, and the second requires $k=n\pi$ for a positive <integer> $n$. Thus $\boxed{\lambda_{\min}=\pi^2}$.

The admissible trial function $y=\sin\pi x$ has quotient
$$
\frac{\int_0^1\pi^2\cos^2\pi x\,dx}{\int_0^1\sin^2\pi x\,dx}=\pi^2.
$$
Therefore the minimum, or infimum before its attainment is established, is $\boxed{\leq\pi^2}$ as requested. The sharp <Poincaré inequality> supplies the reverse inequality on the finite-energy zero-trace class, so the exact minimum is also $\pi^2$ and is attained by this trial function. The one-sided assertion in the source does not require a full variational existence argument.

